<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#bull-leaping">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bull-leaping</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Taurokathapsia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bull-leaping is an acrobatic feat in which a performer vaults over a charging bull, shown above all in the art of Minoan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, where it is thought to have been a key ritual of the elite and perhaps of religion. Leapers, possibly both men and women, are shown grasping the horns or somersaulting over the bull's back, though whether the images record a real performance is debated. Similar scenes occur in Hittite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and the feat survives as a sport in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bull-leaping.jpg" alt="Bull-leaping" width="320" height="173" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gleb Simonov · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Knossos_bull_leaping_fresco.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
